        {
            "id": "slide-1",
            "title": "The Three Time Axes: Past, Present & Future",
            "status": "uncompleted",
            "state": "active",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "heading-1-1",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "⏳ Understanding the Three Primary Time Axes",
                        "level": 1,
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-1",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "In English grammar, every event occurs along a linear <strong>time axis</strong>. The <strong>Simple Tenses</strong> are our primary tools for anchoring actions to a specific point on that line: <em>Past</em> (already happened), <em>Present</em> (happening now or regularly), and <em>Future</em> (yet to happen).",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "poll-1-1",
                    "type": "poll",
                    "props": {
                        "question": "Which time axis does the word 'Yesterday' anchor a sentence to?",
                        "options": [
                            {
                                "id": "opt1",
                                "text": "Past Time Axis"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt2",
                                "text": "Present Time Axis"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "opt3",
                                "text": "Future Time Axis"
                            }
                        ],
                        "points": 5,
                        "state": "active"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "paragraph-1-2",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "They are called <strong>Simple Tenses</strong> because they express single, complete states or actions without adding continuous (-ing) or perfect (has/had) extensions. Let's test your ability to match time adverbs to their correct time axis:",
                        "align": "left"
                    },
                    "state": "active",
                    "status": "uncompleted"
                },
                {
                    "id": "matchingPairs-1-1",
                    "type": "matchingPairs",
                    "props": {
                        "title": "Match each time signal word to its corresponding Time Axis.",
                        "pairs": [
                            {
                                "id": "p1",
                                "left": "Yesterday / Last week / In 1969",
                                "right": "Past Tense Axis"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "p2",
                                "left": "Every day / Currently / Always",
                                "right": "Present Tense Axis"
                            },
                            {
                                "id": "p3",
